/-!
# A local criterion for N-1 domains

Source: Stacks Project, Algebra, Lemma 10.161.15 (Tag 0333), implication (1)+(2) to N-1
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-n1-local-characterization).
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u v

/-- A Noetherian domain is N-1 if some nonzero principal localization is normal and every
maximal localization is N-1. Source: Stacks Project, Algebra, Lemma 10.161.15 (Tag 0333).
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-n1-local-characterization)

Proof, following the node's eight steps:
1. Write A' for the integral closure in K. The normal ring A_f is a normal domain,
   with fraction field K (lem-weil-pbc-normal-domain-of-local-normal). Consider the
   intermediate rings A ⊆ B ⊆ A' finite over A. They are Noetherian domains with fraction
   field K by lem-weil-pbc-finite-algebra-noetherian.
2. Integral closure commutes with localization
   (lem-weil-pbc-integral-closure-commutes-localization), so B_f is integral over A_f
   inside K. Since A_f is integrally closed, B_f = A_f.
3. The non-normal locus in Spec B is closed by lem-weil-pbc-normal-locus-open. Its
   image Z_B in Spec A is closed by lem-weil-pbc-integral-spec-closed-map. A prime p
   avoids Z_B exactly when all B_q over p are normal domains.
4. At each maximal m, N-1 gives finitely many generators of the normalization of A_m.
   Integral-closure-commutes-localization writes them as x_j/s_j with x_j in A' and
   s_j outside m. Set B = A[x_j], finite by lem-weil-pbc-finite-of-integral-generators.
   Then B localized outside m is that normalization, a normal domain by
   lem-weil-pbc-integral-closure-algebraic-extension-normal. Each B_q over m is a
   localization of this normal domain, hence normal by lem-weil-pbc-localization-normal-domain.
   Thus m avoids Z_B.
5. These complements cover Spec A: every prime lies in a maximal ideal
   (lem-weil-pbc-proper-ideal-contained-in-maximal), and a closed set containing that
   prime contains its specializations. Quasi-compactness
   (lem-weil-pbc-affine-scheme-qcqs) gives finitely many B_i with empty intersection of Z_B_i.
6. The ring B generated by all B_i is finite over A, since their finitely many integral
   generators generate B (lem-weil-pbc-finite-of-integral-generators).
7. For each q of B, some B_i has normal localization at q ∩ B_i. Localizing B at the
   complement of q ∩ B_i gives an integral subring of K over that integrally closed
   domain, hence the same ring (lem-weil-pbc-integral-closure-commutes-localization).
   B_q is a further localization, so is normal (lem-weil-pbc-localization-normal-domain).
8. Therefore B is a normal domain (lem-weil-pbc-normal-domain-of-local-normal).
   Every element of A' is integral over B and lies in K, hence belongs to B. Thus A' = B,
   finite as an A-module, which is IsN1 A K (def-weil-pbc-japanese-domain). -/
theorem isN1_of_local_characterization (A : Type u) (K : Type v)
    [CommRing A] [IsDomain A] [IsNoetherianRing A]
    [Field K] [Algebra A K] [IsFractionRing A K]
    (hAway : ∃ f : A, f ≠ 0 ∧
      ∀ (p : Ideal (Localization.Away f)) [p.IsPrime],
        IsDomain (Localization.AtPrime p) ∧ IsIntegrallyClosed (Localization.AtPrime p))
    (hMaximal : ∀ (m : Ideal A) [m.IsMaximal],
      IsN1 (Localization.AtPrime m) (FractionRing (Localization.AtPrime m))) :
    IsN1 A K := by
  sorry
